oints, ordering independence, and coverage remain equivalent. Do not skip, shorten away, serialize around, or weaken assertions, corruption/rollback/race cases, expected-red controls, or production paths; do not mask the issue with -timeout, test sharding outside the repository contract, cached pass results, or -run exclusions. Target cmd/curator <=480s on a clean unloaded -count=1 run, then require two consecutive foreground go test -count=1 ./... runs with the standard default timeout and real exit 0, plus focused global-status tests/coverage, build/vet/gofmt/diff checks, and disk before/after evidence. Do not clear shared Go caches, overlap the two decisive runs with another Go suite, install host software, stage, commit, publish, pin, or edit the separate TASK-260720-1nlmvv worktree. Attach exact optimization and gate evidence and hand off for independent review.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260729-3bd9db, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260729-3bd9db)\nDEVELOPER RUN-260729-3bd9db (performance rework): diagnostic complete, optimization NOT applied, blocked on an ownership/architecture decision. Artifact: TASK-260729-2kaopg_performance-diagnostic.md.\n\nCONSTRAINT: the cmd/curator aggregate duration is not caused by anything in the cmd/curator test setup. Bounded diagnostic go test -count=1 -timeout 30m -json ./cmd/curator exited 0 at 533.463s; 15 of 62 tests take 531.1s and every one of them drives a compiled installation. CPU profile of one compiled-install test attributes 8.24s of 13.18s samples (62.5%) to internal/godriver.fingerprintToolchain: os.(*Root).Open 3.61s, fs.WalkDir 3.26s (os.(*Root).Lstat 2.83s), copyWithContext only 1.34s. 84% of that hot path is os.Root path resolution, not reading or hashing. It walks and hashes the whole GOROOT (14502 files) once per read-only plan (godriver.Probe, deliberately never memoized), once per staging session (Establish), and three more times per staged build (VerifyToolchain at build.go:148/219/253 and plan.go:252). Measured atoms: one compiled-scope status = ~3.1s, one real compiled install = ~12.5s, the same scope without a compiled command = 1.08s for a full install plus four status calls.\n\nFAILED ASSUMPTION: the directive assumed repeated expensive setup inside cmd/curator. There is none left. Immutable setup is already shared - the two largest tests build one fixture and one installation and share them across 14 and 6 subtests - and the residual per-subtest cost is three CLI invocations that ARE the assertions (--json row fields, --check exit code, human line). Nothing is memoizable: the toolchain fingerprint is deliberately not, and the operation-private GOCACHE is a hermeticity property. Packaging cannot be split: the tests live in package main, call run() in-process, and assert on unexported identifiers, so a split requires moving run() into an importable package and rewriting ~1500 lines against string literals, which loses compile-time coupling and fails the equivalent-coverage condition. Trimming a task-owned GOROOT mirror is not viable either: src/ is 11006 of 14502 files and stdlib compilation needs it. No masking route (timeout, sharding, -run exclusion, cached results) was used.\n\nOPTION QUANTIFIED: a directory-scoped walk of fingerprintToolchain - descend through per-directory *os.Root handles and reuse the handle of the directory both phases are inside, keeping the identical record set, sort and byte stream - computes the IDENTICAL sha256 and is 2.2x faster (1.64s -> 0.72s per GOROOT, three rounds, identical_digest=true). Prototype in .temp/TASK-260729-2kaopg/probe/main.go; no worktree file was modified. Project